      "rev_n_1_e633d7_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_e633d7_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_e633d7",
        "thought_text": "Sort the hills by height and consider the tallest hills first for building houses. This ensures that we are maximizing the number of suitable hills with minimal height adjustments.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410427.647479
      },
      "rev_n_2_133d70_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_133d70_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_133d70",
        "thought_text": "For each potential number of houses k, simulate the process of decreasing the heights of hills to make exactly k hills suitable for construction. Track the minimum number of height decreases required for each k.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410427.647487
      },
      "rev_n_3_ce21ec_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_ce21ec_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_ce21ec",
        "thought_text": "Use dynamic programming to efficiently calculate the minimum number of height decreases needed for each k. Define a DP table where dp[i][j] represents the minimum number of height decreases needed to make j hills suitable among the first i hills.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410427.647491
      },
      "rev_n_4_7fad99_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_7fad99_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_7fad99",
        "thought_text": "The current approach calculates the differences between consecutive hills and uses a max-heap to determine the minimum hours required for each k. However, it might not correctly account for the requirement that a hill must be strictly higher than its neighbors. We need to ensure that we are correctly identifying and handling the peaks in the sequence.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1308887403658561948",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410676.7004201
      },
      "rev_n_5_29c32c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_29c32c_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_29c32c",
        "thought_text": "Instead of focusing solely on differences between consecutive hills, consider a dynamic programming approach where we maintain a table to track the minimum hours required to create k valid peaks up to each hill. This would allow us to more accurately account for the constraints and find the optimal solution.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1308887403658561948",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410676.7004669
      },
      "rev_n_6_9d89de_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_9d89de_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_9d89de",
        "thought_text": "An alternative strategy could involve sorting the hills by their heights and then calculating the minimum hours required to create k valid peaks by iteratively removing the smallest differences between hills. This approach might simplify the problem by allowing us to focus on the most critical differences first.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "1308887403658561948",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410676.700475
      },
      "rev_n_7_0b897c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_0b897c_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_0b897c",
        "thought_text": "Consider using a greedy approach to identify potential hill candidates for building houses by iterating through the hills and keeping track of local maxima. This can help in efficiently calculating the minimum adjustments needed for different values of k.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1220001194842560860",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410718.592607
      },
      "rev_n_8_b696f3_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_b696f3_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_b696f3",
        "thought_text": "Implement a dynamic programming solution where the state dp[i][j] represents the minimum time required to make the first i hills suitable for building j houses. This approach can systematically explore all possibilities and find optimal solutions for each k.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-1220001194842560860",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794410718.5926201
      },
      "rev_n_9_3c2643_0": {
